t of <Picture 1>.\n\nThe camera closely reproduces the distinctive perspective of <Picture 1>: a strong high-angle first-person smartphone POV looking substantially downward at the woman. The camera is clearly above her eye level. The top of her head, shoulders and upper body are visibly seen from above. Keep the neon signage visible in the upper part of the frame. Do not use an eye-level view, third-person view, orbiting camera or cuts.\n\nA single adult arm belonging to the camera operator enters naturally from the lower-right foreground. Show a connected forearm, wrist, palm and fingers. The hand is a moderate-size foreground element, not a giant object. Keep the hand primarily within the lower-right quadrant, occupying approximately 10–18% of the image area. The hand must never extend across the center, cover the woman's face or cover her torso. The woman remains the primary visual subject and the hand is only the foreground controller.\n\nKeep the woman physically close to the smartphone, approximately 1.0–1.2 meters away. Her full body occupies approximately 70–80% of the vertical frame. Do not pull the camera backward, do not place her deep into the street and do not progressively make her smaller.\n\nThe foreground index finger directly controls the woman's dance movement. The woman does not independently freestyle. The hand is the visible movement cue and the woman's body immediately follows it.\n\nMaintain these exact control relationships throughout the entire video:\n\nUP → WOMAN RISES.\nDOWN → WOMAN LOWERS.\nLEFT → WOMAN MOVES LEFT.\nRIGHT → WOMAN MOVES RIGHT.\n\nFor UP, the index finger moves clearly upward and the woman's body immediately rises. Her torso extends upward, her posture becomes taller, her arms snap into a lifted dance pose and her center of gravity moves upward.\n\nFor DOWN, the index finger moves clearly downward and the woman's body immediately lowers. Her knees bend into a low groove position, hips drop, center of gravity sinks and torso settles into a controlled bounce.\n\nFor LEFT, the index finger moves toward screen-left and the woman's body immediately shifts and glides toward screen-left. Her weight, torso and hips follow the same direction, feet stepping crisply with the beat. The entire body movement must be clearly visible.\n\nFor RIGHT, the index finger moves toward screen-right and the woman's body immediately shifts and glides toward screen-right. Her weight, torso and hips follow the same direction, feet stepping crisply with the beat. The entire body movement must be clearly visible.\n\nLEFT and RIGHT must not become tiny hip twitches. The woman's body must visibly travel from side to side. Keep the horizontal movement simple so the connection between finger direction and body direction remains unmistakable.\n\nThe hand gesture and corresponding body movement are tightly synchronized. The woman's movement begins as the corresponding finger gesture begins and develops within the same musical beat. No noticeable reaction delay, no one-beat delay, no anticipation and no independent movement.\n\nThe dance is a confident, stylish K-pop street dance challenge: crisp rhythmic footwork, sharp body isolations, bouncy groove, precise arm poses, cool playful facial expression, direct eye contact and natural hair movement. The dance should feel like a polished viral social-media dance challenge set on a neon night street. Make the movement energetic, sharp, rhythmic and visually captivating while keeping it tasteful.\n\nDuring LEFT and RIGHT movements, maintain the direct horizontal hand control while adding clean weight shifts and a light hip bounce. During UP and DOWN movements, maintain the direct vertical hand control while adding smooth body waves and sharp pose hits. Never sacrifice hand-to-body control for complicated choreography.\n\nUse a fast modern K-pop dance rhythm with strong clear beats. The ten commands play back-to-back, each command lasting approximately 0.5–0.6 seconds, for a total runtime of approximately 5–6 seconds in one continuous take.\n\nCommand sequence:\nRIGHT → LEFT → DOWN → UP → LEFT → RIGHT → UP → DOWN → RIGHT → LEFT.\n\nEach command produces one clearly visible corresponding body movement. Do not add unrelated freestyle movements, spins, walking or complicated footwork.\n\nThe index finger moves with strong, deliberate gestures. The wrist and forearm naturally follow the finger. Use short, clear upward, downward, leftward and rightward gestures. Do not use weak floating gestures. Keep the hand in the lower-right foreground and never allow it to dominate the center of the frame.\n\nMaintain one continuous handheld smartphone recording with subtle natural camera instability. Preserve the strong high-angle first-person POV, the neon night street environment, the woman's face, hairstyle, costume and proportions, and the foreground hand placement consistently through the entire video. No cuts, no zooms, no camera transitions.\n"], "inputs": [{"widget": {"name": "prompt"}, "name": "prompt", "label": "prompt", "type": "STRING", "localized_name": "提示词"}], "flags": {}, "id": 36, "type": "CR Prompt Text", "properties": {"widget_ue_connectable": {}, "Node name for S&R": "CR Prompt Text"}, "order": 16}, {"mode": 0, "outputs": [{"name": "Filenames", "label": "文件名", "type": "VHS_FILENAMES", "localized_name": "Filenames"}], "size": [430, 1085.04345703125], "pos": [2299.50830078125, 28.38155746459961], "widgets_values": {"save_output": true, "filename_prefix": "MiniMaxH3/stable_4v4a", "loop_count": 0, "pix_fmt": "yuv420p", "save_metadata": true, "crf": 19, "trim_to_audio": false, "videopreview": {"paused": false, "hidden": false, "params": {"storageProvider": "tos", "cos_url": "https://rh-comfyui01.tos-cn-beijing.volces.com/f70f904ceca88d425354d34eb8ea67a8/output/MiniMaxH3/stable_4v4a_00001_p80-audio_rgsaq_1791359355.mp4", "workflow": "stable_4v4a_00001_p80.png", "format": "video/h264-mp4", "subfolder": "MiniMaxH3", "label": "Normal", "ty